Feature: the UI conformance suite: the bar the interface is held to
  Status 2026-10-22: decided. The bevy_ui substrate passed the full tier (69/0/0 at 1 frame p50) where the Dioxus/Blitz
  stack scored 6/38/25; it became the legacy app's only UI and the Dioxus path was deleted (docs/ts-native.feature P25). The
  history below is kept as it was.
  Status 2026-10-21: the contract, written before the suite. Shane, 10-21: "theres lots of problems all over the ui
  everywhere ... responsiveness, parity, layouts, responsive layouts, realtime changes updating immediately". Agreed
  plan: write the bar first, then measure the current stack (lite panels + DOM surfaces on dioxus-native/Blitz) and a
  bevy_ui spike of the feed space against it, then decide on the numbers. "Working" means this suite passes, not that
  it looks fine to someone.

  The suite tests the interface the way a person uses it: real window input in, what the window shows out. It knows
  nothing about lite panels, Blitz or Bevy. Each UI substrate implements the two MCP tools below; every case runs
  unchanged on either one.

  Rule: It never touches a person's work
    Scenario: A run's world is its own
      Then each run starts the app headless (raea-page-host, which has no window) with its own HOME, its own MCP port, and a throwaway
        copy of a fixture workspace (sdk/ui/conformance/fixture: a few renders, stills, a storyboard, a swipe;
        small files, committed or generated by the suite)
      And no case sends anything to an agent: the chat runs on a stub agent that echoes (RAEA_FEED_AGENT=echo or the
        substrate's equivalent), so a typed message and a review note can be checked without a model or a cost
      And the run stops its app and deletes its copy, pass or fail

  Rule: Input goes in as the window gets it
    Scenario: The input tool
      Then MCP `input` takes one event in window points and posts it to the app's window as AppKit would deliver it
        (NSEvents through the window, not a panel's private command): move {x,y}; down/up {x,y,button,clicks};
        leave (the pointer leaves the window); wheel {x,y,dy,unit: "pixel"|"line",phase?} with natural scrolling on
        and off (dy in the device's sign, as macOS hands it); key {key, mods} down/up; text {text} (an inserted
        string); ime {marked, commit} (marked text, then committed); resize {w,h}; and returns the frame number it
        landed before
      And the suite drives clicks, double clicks, drags and hovers only through it

  Rule: What the window shows comes out as a tree
    Scenario: The ui tool
      Then MCP `ui` returns the interface as presented in the latest frame: {frame, window: {w,h}, nodes: [...]}, each
        node {id, role, name, rect: {x,y,w,h} in window points, visible, hovered, focused, pressed, selected, value,
        scroll: {top, max}?, text?}
      And ids are stable test ids the UI declares (data-testid on the current stack; a TestId component in Bevy),
        e.g. left.tab.feeds, left.tab.assets, left.feeds, left.assets, left.feed.<name> (lowercased, dashes; its badge left.feed.<name>.badge),
        feed.list, feed.card.<n>, feed.search, review.back, review.title, review.picture (state playing/paused),
        review.take.<n>/prev/next, review.approve, review.track, review.note, review.board, chat.composer, chat.send,
        chat.transcript, chat.message.<n>, header.toggle.chat, header.toggle.files; and the regions whose
        boxes reflow: left, middle, chat
      And a substrate that composites panels also lists them under `panels`: {name, shown, rect, texture: {w, h,
        scale?}, stale}, the texture it last presented for each
      And the frame number is the presented frame's, the same counter `input` returns, so a latency is frames between
        the event and the first tree that shows its effect
      And a substrate may also answer `frame_png` (the composited window) for the few cases that check pixels

  Rule: Every case is data: steps, an expectation, a budget
    Scenario: A case
      Then a case is a file under sdk/ui/conformance/cases/ naming: its area (pointer, scroll, text, focus, layout,
        responsive, live, panels, review, chat), the window size, the steps (input events and waits on the tree), the
        expectation (predicates on node fields), and a budget in frames (default 2 at 60 Hz for pointer and text, 1 for
        a live update, 3 for a resize or a panel reflow)
      And a case runs the feed (apps/app) on raea-page-host at its `route` (default /feed/f-ides-all) and names the
        feed's own data-testids (feed, card-*, search, feed-f-*, menu-*, detail-*); a node's aria-current is its
        `current` field, and a boolean data-* flag (data-selected) a state token of its name
      And sdk/ui/conformance/fake-cases/ holds the legacy app's cases, whose tree the runner's fake serves: they test
        the runner, not an app
      And the runner takes the start snapshot, each snap, and each step's targets from a settled tree: no panel stale
        and the same rects on two consecutive frames (stale panels unchanged for 1.5 s count, noted in the result),
        within a bounded wait (a tree that never settles fails the case); that wait comes before the input, so it
        never counts toward a latency
      And a result is pass, fail (the expectation never held: what the tree showed instead), or slow (it held, after
        the budget: by how many frames)
      And the runner prints one line per case and writes results.jsonl; it exits 1 on any fail

  Rule: The first cases are what's broken today (the 10-21 audit and Shane's reports)
    Scenario: Pointer
      Then a card under the pointer is hovered, and stops being hovered when the pointer leaves it or the window
      And a press on a feed row in the left panel selects that row and no other (hit-test matches what's drawn)
      And a double click on a card opens its review once and pins no note
      And a press on review.back always goes back, whatever buttons the bar has
      And two quick presses on header.toggle.chat open and close the chat and never resize the window
    Scenario: Scroll
      Then a pixel wheel and a line wheel both scroll the feed, the left panel's Assets list and the chat transcript, in the
        direction macOS's natural-scrolling setting says, by at least the distance the device moved (a line ≥ 32 pt)
      And a scroll stops at the content's end (a storyboard has a bottom) and scrolls only what's under the pointer
    Scenario: Text and focus
      Then a press in feed.search or chat.composer focuses it; typed text, Backspace, Esc and Enter do what they say,
        with no key lost however fast, including the first after the press
      And IME marked text shows while composing and commits once
      And with a note open, a press anywhere else commits or drops the note and the press still does what it hit
    Scenario: Layout and responsive
      Then at 1440x900, 1180x820, 900x700 and 700x600 nothing overlaps that shouldn't (no two visible interactive
        nodes intersect), no text is cut to zero width, every interactive node is at least 24 pt and on screen
      And below a breakpoint the side panels give way (collapse or overlay) rather than squeezing the middle under 360 pt
      And take buttons that don't fit fold rather than covering the title
    Scenario: Panels open and close
      Then toggling the chat or the left panel reflows the middle within the budget: its rect and its content's rects
        follow (no stale frame of the old size past the budget)
      And toggling the chat and the files in rounds leaves every shown panel presenting a texture its box's
        size, none stale, after each toggle (10-21: half the toggles drew the other parked size; the dock left with the
        feed direction, Shane 10-22)
    Scenario: Live
      Then a new item arriving in the feed (a file written into the fixture's renders/) shows in the feed and its feed's
        badge within 1 frame of the app noticing it (and the app notices within 1 s)
      And a message arriving in the chat transcript shows the frame it arrives, and the transcript scrolls rather than
        squashing
    Scenario: Review
      Then dragging review.track scrubs (the value follows the drag) and a paused video stays paused after
      And J after Approve opens the next item, not the first; Esc goes back

  Rule: The suite runs in CI and decides
    Scenario: CI
      Then bench/ci.sh runs the suite in its quick tier on the m5pro runner against the current build, and the nightly
        full tier at every responsive size
      And each substrate's run is recorded side by side (pass count, fail count, p50/p95 frames per area)
    Scenario: The decision
      Then the bevy_ui spike (the legacy app's bevy_ui mode, the feed space only) and the current stack run the same cases; if the
        spike passes more with lower latency, the shell is ported space by space, each port gated by the suite, and the
        old panels stay until the new ones reach parity
      And every new UI failure anyone finds becomes a case before it's fixed
